    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the downstream effects of that? Specifically, how does the decrease in Angiotensin II affect aldosterone levels and sodium/water retention? I want to make sure I have the full feedback loop down for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The reduction in Angiotensin II directly leads to decreased aldosterone secretion from the adrenal glands. Since aldosterone normally promotes sodium and water reabsorption in the kidneys, this decrease results in natriuresis and diuresis. Consequently, the body excretes more sodium and water, which reduces overall plasma volume and further contributes to decreased blood pressure. This disruption effectively interrupts the RAAS's ability to maintain vasoconstriction and fluid retention.",
      "source": "target",
