    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So basically the whole thing is a cascade of inhibition. If you had to explain the \"interruption\" part of that loop to someone in a way that's easy to visualize—like a metaphor for how the signaling gets stopped—how would you describe it? Just trying to find a way to make the concept stick in my head better.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine the RAAS pathway as a continuous assembly line where Angiotensin II is the final product—a strong signal to constrict blood vessels and hold onto salt. ACE inhibitors act like a crucial stop sign placed right before the final assembly station. By blocking ACE, the pathway stalls, preventing the production of Angiotensin II, which is the main signal that keeps the \"line\" producing high blood pressure. This effectively takes the brakes off the system, allowing blood pressure to drop.",
      "source": "target",
